from assemble_topic import assemble
FACTS={
'01':('Domestic, agricultural, industrial and ecological needs can compete for a limited usable water supply.','Water allocation requires consistent units, periods and recognition of quality, access and delivery constraints.'),
'01-01':('Agriculture uses water for crops, animals and relevant operations, with irrigation distinct from rain-fed moisture.','Withdrawal, application and net consumption are different measures of agricultural use.'),
'01-02':('Domestic water supports drinking, cooking, hygiene, sanitation and cleaning.','A connection count does not establish adequate quality, timing, affordability or usable service for every household.'),
'01-03':('Industrial water needs vary with processing, cooling and cleaning operations.','Suitable reuse can reduce fresh supply needs where quality and operating requirements are met.'),
'01-04':('Combined demand can exceed usable supply even when a source contains water.','Lower use per person can coexist with higher total demand if the served population increases sufficiently.'),
'02':('Groundwater occurs in pores and fractures while surface water occurs above ground in channels and stores.','Their connections through infiltration, recharge and discharge require an actual water balance rather than source labels alone.'),
'02-01':('An aquifer can store and transmit useful groundwater, reached by suitable extraction works.','A drilled borehole does not establish adequate sustainable yield or suitable quality.'),
'02-02':('Rivers and reservoirs are surface-water sources whose available volume varies with flows, use and losses.','A reservoir’s maximum capacity is different from its current content or daily treatment capacity.'),
'03':('Usable water supports settlements, farming, services and enterprises through quantity, quality and delivery.','Diversified supplies can help resilience without eliminating maintenance, treatment and sustainable resource limits.'),
'04':('The hydrological cycle transfers water among atmosphere, land, surface stores and groundwater through distinct processes.','Floods and droughts affect usable supplies through different inflow, loss, contamination and infrastructure pathways.'),
'04-01':('Rainfall is distributed among interception, evaporation, soil storage, runoff and deeper movement under the relevant conditions.','Recharge and rainfall at a surface point need not occur at the same rate or time.'),
'04-02':('Heavy input and rapid routing or restricted drainage can produce inundation.','Floodwater can coexist with unsafe or interrupted household supply.'),
'04-03':('Sustained water deficits can lower river inflow, storage and groundwater reserves over different timescales.','One later rainfall event need not restore a previously depleted reserve.'),
'05':('Surface storage, groundwater extraction, desalination and reclamation have different resource and operating conditions.','Supply methods must be connected to usable quality, delivery and demand before adequacy is established.'),
'05-01':('A dam is a structure while its impounded reservoir occupies the upstream side of the usual valley arrangement.','Reservoir usefulness depends on inflow, storage, losses, quality and environmental or social effects.'),
'05-02':('Wells and boreholes provide groundwater access rather than creating a natural inflow.','Extraction requires suitable sustainable yield, quality, energy and maintenance.'),
'05-03':('Desalination removes dissolved salts through suitable processes requiring energy and equipment.','Saline-source access does not eliminate delivery, brine-management or affordability constraints.'),
'05-04':('Water reuse requires treatment appropriate to the next intended use and reliable quality control.','Windhoek combines reclamation with other sources rather than treating untreated wastewater as directly suitable drinking water.'),
'06':('Pollution explanation needs a source, pathway and affected use or environmental function.','Clear appearance and a generic treatment label do not establish suitability for drinking.'),
'06-01':('Sewage, nutrient runoff, unsuitable industrial discharge and spills can contaminate water through different pathways.','Proximity of an activity alone does not establish its actual harmful discharge.'),
'06-02':('Unsafe microbial water exposure can transmit illness, while pollution can raise treatment or alternative-supply costs.','A geographic pollution record is not an individual medical diagnosis or complete causal attribution.'),
'06-03':('Excess nutrient enrichment can promote growth whose later decay consumes dissolved oxygen.','Sediment, toxic substances and oxygen changes affect environments through different mechanisms.')}
CARDS={
'001':('Explain how a new water user can create competition where existing needs nearly fill a fixed usable supply.','The new withdrawal increases combined demand. If the total exceeds usable supply, some need remains unmet without demand changes, extra supply or changed timing. Allocation then affects households, production and relevant environmental functions.',['1 mark for added demand as a factor; 1 for the combined-demand link; 1 for the supply gap; 1 for its conditional allocation effect.']),
'002':('Describe the daily account: usable supply 100 m³; domestic demand 50, agriculture 40 and industry 30 m³, with no extra source or reserve release.','Combined demand is 120 m³/day, twenty above supply. Agriculture and industry together require seventy, while domestic needs are fifty. The resource establishes a gap, not its distribution among users.',['1 mark for the total/gap relationship; 1 for sector evidence; 1 for the allocation limitation.']),
'003':('Name one agricultural water use other than deliberate crop irrigation.','Livestock drinking or a suitable farming operation.',['1 mark for a relevant agricultural-use name.']),
'004':('Name one essential domestic water use.','Drinking, cooking, hygiene or sanitation.',['1 mark for a relevant domestic-use name.']),
'005':('Name one industrial water use.','Process water, cooling or suitable equipment cleaning.',['1 mark for a relevant industrial-use name.']),
'006':('Name the management problem where several users’ combined requirements exceed usable water supply.','Competing demands or a water-allocation shortage.',['1 mark for a relevant problem name.']),
'007':('Name the two broad source categories represented by an aquifer and a river.','Groundwater and surface water respectively.',['1 mark for the complete paired category-name point.']),
'008':('Describe the source contrast: A is an aquifer reached by a borehole; B is a reservoir, and neither record supplies quality tests or operating yields.','A stores groundwater in suitable material and B stores surface water. The borehole is an access point, while the reservoir is a surface store. Their existence alone does not establish safe quality or dependable usable output.',['1 mark for the source-category relationship; 1 for access/store evidence; 1 for the quality/yield limit.']),
'009':('Name material capable of storing and transmitting useful groundwater.','An aquifer.',['1 mark for the term name aquifer.']),
'010':('Name a surface-water store created by impounding a river.','A reservoir.',['1 mark for the store name reservoir.']),
'011':('Explain how a water source can retain water while a settlement temporarily loses access.','A pump, treatment or delivery interruption can break the source-to-user chain. Water remains in the source but cannot reach users in the required form or time. The response must address the interrupted stage rather than assume physical exhaustion.',['1 mark for infrastructure interruption as a factor; 1 for the chain link; 1 for the usable-access effect; 1 for the distinction from depleted source stock.']),
'012':('Describe the service record: a source holds water, but the only delivery pump fails and no alternative is available.','The store exists while delivery is interrupted. The immediate constraint is the pump stage, not demonstrated disappearance of the source. The evidence does not establish when service will resume without repair information.',['1 mark for the stock/service relationship; 1 for pump evidence; 1 for the restoration-evidence limit.']),
'013':('Explain how repeated low inflows can create a supply problem even if the first dry season is buffered by a reservoir.','Existing storage initially supplies withdrawals. If inflow stays below abstraction and other losses, stock declines across intervals. The reserve can eventually constrain usable delivery, so a later rainfall event may not restore the earlier balance.',['1 mark for low inflow as a factor; 1 for the stock/use link; 1 for cumulative depletion; 1 for its later supply effect.']),
'014':('Describe the supplied reservoir-balance diagram: opening stock 100 units, inflow 20, abstraction 30 and evaporation 10 over one interval; other changes are absent.','Inflow adds water while abstraction and evaporation remove it. The closing stock is eighty, a twenty-unit decline. The diagram establishes this balance, not a meteorological drought without a rainfall baseline.',['1 mark for the arrow/flow relationship; 1 for the closing-stock evidence; 1 for the drought-attribution limitation.']),
'015':('Name the process by which water enters the ground from the surface.','Infiltration.',['1 mark for the process name infiltration.']),
'016':('Name the inundation hazard when water covers land normally not submerged.','Flooding.',['1 mark for the hazard name flooding.']),
'017':('Name the sustained water-shortage condition relative to the setting or relevant requirements.','Drought.',['1 mark for the condition name drought.']),
'018':('Name the supply method removing dissolved salts from saline water.','Desalination.',['1 mark for the method name desalination.']),
'019':('How far do you agree that a new borehole alone proves an adequate safe supply? Drilling is complete, but no sustainable-yield test, quality result, pump or delivery record is supplied.','Disagree that the service is established. The access work exists, but usable quantity, quality and delivery remain unverified. Suitable testing, equipment and operation are needed; drilling does not itself create recharge or a complete supply chain.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using drilling evidence and the missing yield, quality and delivery conditions.','1 C mark for a supported decision against established adequate supply.']),
'020':('Name the structure holding back flow, distinct from the stored water body.','A dam or dam wall.',['1 mark for the structure name dam.']),
'021':('Name a drilled access point used to reach groundwater.','A borehole.',['1 mark for the access-point name borehole.']),
'022':('Name one practical input needed for desalination besides saline water.','Energy, appropriate equipment or a suitable operating/maintenance service.',['1 mark for a relevant input name.']),
'023':('Name the approach treating used water for a suitable further use.','Water recycling, reuse or reclamation.',['1 mark for a relevant approach name.']),
'024':('Explain a pathway from excess nutrient runoff to harm for aquatic organisms.','Nutrients entering water can stimulate abundant growth. When this material dies, decomposition consumes dissolved oxygen. Reduced oxygen can harm susceptible aquatic organisms under those conditions.',['1 mark for nutrient loading as a factor; 1 for the growth link; 1 for decay consuming oxygen; 1 for the aquatic effect.']),
'025':('Describe the evidence: water looks clearer after solids settle, but no microbial or dissolved-chemical tests are supplied.','Visible suspended material has decreased. The observation does not establish removal of microbes or dissolved contaminants. The resource supports a clarity improvement, not drinking-water suitability.',['1 mark for the clarity pattern; 1 for settling/missing-test evidence; 1 for the bounded safety interpretation.']),
'026':('Name a contamination pathway where untreated sewage reaches a water source.','An unsuitable sewage discharge or sanitation failure.',['1 mark for a relevant pollution-source/pathway name.']),
'027':('Name one human consequence of water contamination besides an ecological effect.','Illness risk from relevant exposure, increased treatment cost or loss of suitable water access.',['1 mark for a relevant human-effect name.']),
'028':('Name the water condition that can harm aquatic life after decomposition uses much of its dissolved oxygen.','Low dissolved oxygen or oxygen depletion.',['1 mark for the condition name oxygen depletion.']),
'029':('Explain the error in placing the impounded reservoir south of a wall on a river flowing north to south. What direction test locates it?','The confusion places stored upstream water downstream. Flow approaches the wall from the north, so the impounded reservoir backs into the northern upstream valley. Follow the incoming-flow direction to discriminate retained storage from released downstream water.',['1 mark for the upstream/downstream error; 1 for the correct north/upstream distinction and flow-direction test.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','02','05'],'prompt':'(a) Name the source category reached by a groundwater borehole. [1]\n(b) Describe three different water uses across domestic, agricultural and industrial activity. [3]\n(c) Name the process removing salts from saline water. [1]\n(d) Describe this account: daily usable supply 100 m³ and combined demand 120 m³, with no reserve release or extra source. [2]','answer':'(a) Groundwater. (b) Domestic drinking/cooking or hygiene; agricultural crop or livestock needs; industrial cooling/processing or cleaning. (c) Desalination. (d) Demand is twenty m³/day above supply; all stated needs cannot simultaneously be fully met from that supply without a change.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for groundwater.','(b) 3 A marks for three distinct sector-linked use point descriptions.','(c) 1 A mark for desalination.','(d) 2 B marks for the quantified gap and its supply-demand interpretation.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['01','04','05'],'prompt':'A hypothetical reservoir starts an interval with 100 water units, receives twenty, supplies thirty to users and loses ten to evaporation. Other changes are absent; no rainfall baseline is given.\n(a) Name the loss process changing liquid water into atmospheric vapour. [1]\n(b) Calculate closing storage. [2]\n(c) Describe three features of the stock/flow account. [3]\n(d) What judgement is supported about the claim “this interval alone proves meteorological drought”? [1]','answer':'(a) Evaporation. (b) 100+20−30−10 = 80 units. (c) Inflow is twenty; combined outflow is forty, twice inflow; stock falls twenty units or 20% of its opening value. Credit three distinct relevant observations, without treating equivalent expressions of the same decline as separate points. (d) Drought is not established without the relevant rainfall baseline and sustained-period evidence.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for evaporation.','(b) 2 B marks for the full stock-plus-inflow-minus-losses method and correct closing value/unit.','(c) 3 B marks for distinct input, output and stock-change patterns supported by the resource data.','(d) 1 C mark for the supported judgement distinguishing a water balance from a meteorological diagnosis.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Calculate',2,'B'),('c','Describe',3,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['01','03','05'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that Windhoek’s use of several water sources makes demand management unnecessary? The city’s documented approach combines surface supplies, groundwater and reclamation. Quantities, treatment, delivery and future inflows remain limited or variable; no unlimited reserve is stated. Judge dependable usable supply. [5]','answer':'Disagree. A mix can diversify opportunities, but each source still has quantity, quality, operating and delivery conditions. Shared maintenance or energy constraints can also affect access. Demand management and loss reduction can help maintain useful service alongside supply measures. The existence of several sources does not establish that every future demand is met. A current judgement needs matched reserve, flow and demand records.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the case evidence about its source mix, resource/service limits and the complementary role of demand or loss management.','1 C mark for a supported decision rejecting automatic adequacy from source count.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]
